<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sacsayhuamán</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Saksaywaman</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sacsayhuamán is an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> complex on a hill overlooking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cusco</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, famous for its zigzag walls of huge, precisely fitted stone blocks. Built mainly in the 15th century, it served ceremonial and military purposes. In 1536 it was the scene of fierce fighting during <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manco-inca">Manco Inca</a>'s siege of the Spanish in Cusco.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sacsayhuaman.jpg" alt="Sacsayhuamán" width="320" height="176" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Diego Delso · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sacsayhuam%C3%A1n,_Cusco,_Per%C3%BA,_2015-07-31,_DD_27.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Sacsayhuamán, or Saksaywaman, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quechua">Quechua</a> for fortress of the royal falcon or hawk, is a citadel on the northern outskirts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cusco</a>, the historic capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca Empire</a>. It stands at an altitude of 3,701 metres on a steep hill with a wide view of the valley to the southeast. Surface pottery suggests that the hilltop was occupied as early as about 900 CE, and it has been theorised that the site was first built on during the Killke period, which preceded the Inca. According to Inca oral history, Túpac Inca recalled that his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pachacuti">Pachacuti</a> had called Cusco the lion city, and a fortress on the plateau to the north was to be its head.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Spanish chroniclers recorded that the fortress was built in the reigns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pachacuti">Pachacuti</a> and his successors, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tupac-inca-yupanqui">Topa Inca Yupanqui</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huayna-capac">Huayna Capac</a>. Pedro Cieza de León wrote in 1553 that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> had the provinces supply 20,000 men, with 4,000 quarrying stones, 6,000 hauling them with leather and cabuya cables and the rest digging foundations and cutting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a>, in rotation. He described stones twelve feet wide and more than twenty long, fitted so closely that a coin could not be put between them. Pedro Pizarro found a strong fort with two very high round towers and rooms filled with arms, lances, arrows and shields.</p><p>After the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cusco</a> the Spaniards used the site as a quarry and demolished much of it block by block to build the colonial city. Garcilaso de la Vega wrote that hardly a house in Cusco was not made of its stone. Today mostly the stones too large to move remain.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The best-known zone includes the great plaza and three massive terrace walls. The longest wall is about 400 metres and they stand about 6 metres tall, built of stones among the largest used in pre-Hispanic America. The largest limestone block is estimated at between 128 and almost 200 tonnes, and the stones were moved an estimated 35 km from Rumicolca. The close fit, rounded corners, interlocking shapes and inward lean of the walls are thought to have helped them survive earthquakes. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cusco</a> and Sacsayhuamán were listed together as a World Heritage Site in 1983. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inti-raymi">Inti Raymi</a>, the festival of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winter-solstice">winter solstice</a>, is held near the site on 24 June, and Warachikuy on the third Sunday of September.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sacsayhuamán” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Near Cusco, the great walls of <b>Sacsayhuamán</b>, built of fitted stone blocks, served ceremonial as well as defensive purposes.</blockquote><a href="/empire/inca-empire">Inca Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Spanish authorities banned it in the 16th century; it was revived as a public spectacle in 1944 and is now staged each June at <b>Sacsayhuamán</b>.</blockquote><a href="/essays/midwinter">Midwinter</a></li></ul></section>
